WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.400
You may have heard
the expression

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.240
“knowledge is power.”

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.040
Well, today we’re going
to give you more power

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.800
to control your
diet and lifestyle

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.960
by giving you the facts.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.760
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.240
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.680
Parkinson’s is one of the fastest growing
neurological conditions in the world

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.800
and though there is currently
no cure for the disease,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.560
there are dietary changes

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.121
that can significantly improve
symptoms of the disease.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.320
And we start today with the
potential benefits of coffee.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.680
When James Parkinson first described
the classical symptoms of the disease,

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.560
he could hardly foresee the
evolution of our understanding

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.720
over the next two hundred years
like the role of nutrition,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.640
increasing Parkinson's disease
risk with high dietary intakes

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.760
of animal fat, iron, mercury,
and dairy products,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.120
whereas the intake of antioxidants
and a plant-based dietary pattern

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.240
may be protective.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.600
Plant-based diets are known
to preserve body tissues

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.400
from oxidative stress and
inflammation, both hallmarks

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.840
of chronic degenerative
diseases like Parkinson's.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.160
On the contrary, animal-based foods,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.800
particularly rich in animal
proteins and saturated fats,

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.280
are correlated with the promotion
of neurodegenerative diseases,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.120
in addition to some
of the leading killers

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.320
like cancer, heart
disease, diabetes, etc.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.880
Not all plants are necessarily
good though. For example,

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.920
the potential neurotoxicity
of graviola,

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.960
a fruit known as soursop.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.840
Consumption of soursop can lower
blood pressure and uric acid levels

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.640
but may also cause an atypical
form of Parkinson's disease,

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.920
because the fruit contains
neurotoxic compounds.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.400
And indeed, population studies
do show a link between

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.160
the overconsumption of soursop
with neurodegenerative disease.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.720
Yes, those who follow a predominantly
plant-based diet may show

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.240
the lowest prevalence and incidence
of Parkinson's disease,

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.360
but plant-based nutrition is
not just about reducing the risk

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.640
but can be used to
manage the disease.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.800
In my video, "Treating
Parkinson's Disease with Diet,"

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.560
I discussed this case report,
in which a diet low in animal fat

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.720
and including both
whole grains every day,

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.360
as well as one to two cups
of strawberries a day,

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.320
seemed to be effective in reducing
symptoms of Parkinson's disease.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.360
But there are like 20,000
edible plants out there,

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.680
and only a limited number
of them have been studied

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.160
for anti-Parkinson's activity.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.080
One plant that's gotten a lot
of attention is coffee,

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.240
which may exert a
protective effect against

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.880
the development of
Parkinson's disease

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.160
and may even help slow down
the progression of the disease,

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.800
based on studies like this.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.480
They show the Parkinson's patients
who drink coffee or caffeinated tea

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.160
appear to cut their risk
of dying prematurely in half.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.520
But correlation
doesn't mean causation.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:03.040
You don't know if caffeine really
works until you...put it to the test.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.240
Caffeine for the treatment
of Parkinson's disease.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.680
A six-week randomized placebo-
controlled double-blind study.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.920
The caffeine group was started out
at approximately a cup of coffee's

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.600
worth of caffeine twice a day, once
in the morning and once after lunch,

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.480
and then increasing it to two cups'
worth twice a day, which would

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.400
be like four cups of black tea
twice a day or six cups of green.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.800
And a significant improvement
in Parkinson's symptoms

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.360
within three weeks
compared to placebo.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.880
A cheap, safe, simple
treatment for Parkinson's,

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.720
though an important limitation
was the duration of the study.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.880
Caffeine has what's called
a tachyphylactic property,

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:48.800
meaning its effects tend to diminish
over time with repeated use

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.120
(at least when trying
to combat sleepiness).

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.360
So it would be really nice
to see the study repeated

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:01.120
over a longer time frame. But
there was no such longer study

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.440
until five years later.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.360
Randomizing Parkinson's patients
to about two cups of coffee worth

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.000
of caffeine twice a day for
not six weeks, but more than

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:17.840
six months, and no benefit
over placebo. Drats.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.160
Caffeine did not provide sustained
symptomatic benefit after all.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.400
So caffeine may have a short-term
benefit, which quickly dissipates.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.360
Regardless, caffeine
cannot be recommended

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.240
as symptomatic therapy
for Parkinsonism.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:36.560
In our next story,

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.040
we look at how low levels of
neurotoxic chemicals in cheese

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.240
may explain the connection
between dairy product consumption

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:45.680
and Parkinson’s disease.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.120
Four things that we can do that may reduce
our risk of developing Parkinson's disease

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:57.520
is exercise, avoiding dairy products,
pesticides, and avoid getting hit in the head

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.800
(which means wearing your seatbelt,
bike helmet), and—

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.040
if you read journal articles written by
scientists

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.520
with way too much
time on their hands—

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.040
avoid getting attacked
by extraterrestrials,

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.440
a leading cause of traumatic brain injury
in comic books.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:17.840
What about avoiding pesticides and other
industrial pollutants?

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.400
A recent autopsy study
found higher levels in

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:23.520
the brains of Parkinson's
victims of PCBs

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:29.440
found in Monsanto's Aroclor, which was
banned in 1979, but still pollutes the world.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.600
And, the more PCBs found in the brain, the
worse the brain damage.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:39.360
The worst three PCBs appeared to be
numbers 138, 153, and 180—

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.440
the levels of all of which are
significantly lower

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.080
in the bodies of those
eating plant-based diets.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.600
So: "Does a vegan diet reduce risk [of]
Parkinson's disease?"

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:52.320
Good question.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.160
Well, we know that every single prospective
study on dairy products

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:59.920
or milk and Parkinson's disease tended to
find increased risk.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.880
It may be that "dairy
products in the United&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.920
States are contaminated&nbsp;
with neurotoxic chemicals".

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.840
There's substantial evidence
suggesting that

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.520
"exposure to pesticides may increase
Parkinson's disease risk,"

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.040
and autopsies found higher levels of pollutants
and pesticides

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.760
in the brains of Parkinson's
disease patients,

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.600
and some of these toxins are present at low
levels in dairy products.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:25.520
They're talking about toxins like
tetrahydroisoquinoline,

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.360
a Parkinsonism-related compound found
predominantly in cheese.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.720
Although the amounts of this neurotoxin—
even in cheese—are not really high,

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:39.280
the concern is that the chemical may
accumulate in the brain

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.200
over long periods
of consumption.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.600
Finally today, we look at how
plant-based diets in general

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.800
may be used to successfully
treat Parkinson’s disease,

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.280
in part, by boosting
L-DOPA levels.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.400
Caffeine consumption,
both in Asian and Western populations,

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.840
appears to protect against the
development of Parkinson’s disease.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.840
But, what if you already have it?

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.560
A new study found that
giving folks the equivalent

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.840
of about two cups of coffee
a day worth of caffeine

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.240
significantly improved
symptoms of the disease.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.680
Of course, there’s only so much
you can charge for coffee,

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.040
so drug companies took caffeine,
and added a side group

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.960
so they could patent it into new drugs,

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.760
which appear to work no better
than plain caffeine—

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.640
which is dramatically cheaper,
and probably safer.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:28.960
Similarly, other plant foods,
such as berries, may be protective.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:32.960
And, plant-based diets, in general,
may help prevent Parkinson’s.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:35.840
Animal fat and dairy
may increase risk,

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:39.440
whereas “a plant-based diet
dietary pattern may protect against

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:40.480
[Parkinson’s disease].”

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.160
We don’t know if it’s the
animal fat per se, though;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:46.280
it could be the animal protein
that’s increasing risk,

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:49.200
or maybe it’s the dairy,
the mercury in fish,

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.280
the blood-based heme iron,
or less of the protective antioxidants

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.200
in plant foods and
plant-based diets.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.080
We didn’t know,
until recently.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.080
There have been successful case
reports, like this one, in which a dietician

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.040
was struck down with Parkinson’s,
and she was able to clear most of her

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.000
symptoms with a plant-based diet
rich in strawberries, whole wheat,

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.840
and brown rice—rich sources
of these two phytonutrients.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.680
But, there hasn’t been a formal
interventional trial published—until now.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.640
At its root, Parkinson’s is a
dopamine-deficiency disease,

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.720
because of a die-off
of dopamine-generating cells

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:25.840
in the brain.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.400
These cells make
dopamine from L-dopa,

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:30.880
derived from certain amino
acids in our diet.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.080
But, just like we saw
with the serotonin story,

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.760
the consumption of animal protein
may block the transport of L-dopa

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:40.000
into the brain,
crowding it out.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.640
So, at first, researchers tried what’s
called a “protein redistribution diet.”

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.720
Let’s basically only let people
eat meat for supper,

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.960
so that patients are hopefully sleeping
by the time the negative effects kick in.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:57.200
But researchers didn’t consider cutting
out all animal products altogether,

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.000
until it was discovered
that fiber consumption

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.080
naturally boosts L-dopa levels.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:06.960
So, hey, “a plant-based diet,
particularly in its vegan variant,

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.200
is expected to raise levodopa
bioavailability and bring some

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.800
advantages in the management
of [Parkinson’s] disease

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.160
through two mechanisms:

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.080
a reduced protein intake and
an increased fiber intake…”

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:24.400
That’s why plant protein works best,
because that’s where fiber is found.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.760
So, they put people on
a strictly vegan diet,

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.760
keeping beans towards
the end of the day,

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.000
and indeed found a significant
improvement in symptoms.

